WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.640
Speaker 1: Republicans are now desperately&nbsp;
rethinking if and how they should be talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:12.960
about abortion during their campaigns because of&nbsp;
the disastrous flop of the 2022 midterms. Remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:19.380
that in 2022, historically speaking, Republicans&nbsp;
should have done unbelievably well. They should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:24.540
have probably taken control of the Senate, which&nbsp;
they did not. They actually lost ground in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:30.900
Senate going from 50 to 49 seats. They should have&nbsp;
taken control of the House with a much greater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:37.080
margin, but they did not. And now Republicans&nbsp;
are in the situation of having to rethink their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:41.580
strategy. There's an interesting Hill article&nbsp;
by Julia manchester which says Republicans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:47.820
are recalibrating their messaging on abortion&nbsp;
after Democrats successfully used the issue to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:54.145
galvanize their base and win over swing voters in&nbsp;
2022. While the GOP largely focused on the three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.145 --> 00:00:59.220
pronged message of combating rising inflation,&nbsp;
crime and the flow of migrants over the border,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:06.480
exit polls showed abortion was a top priority for&nbsp;
voters at the ballot box. Republican Committee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:11.640
Chair Ronna McDaniel said, quote, It was probably&nbsp;
a bigger factor than a lot of people thought.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:18.900
We've got to get conversant on that. We can't just&nbsp;
do an ostrich method and pretend that it doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:24.000
exist when Democrats are spending $30 million&nbsp;
on that message. According to exit polling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:30.780
27% of voters said abortion was the most important&nbsp;
issue coming in only behind inflation at 31%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:37.980
So there's a few things to understand here.&nbsp;
In general, abortion is a deeply polarizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:43.560
issue. And so there's always a risk when you&nbsp;
campaign on either side of a really polarizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:48.240
issue that you're going to alienate a large swath&nbsp;
of the electorate. That's just a general thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:53.580
with polarizing issues. The issue in particular&nbsp;
with abortion for Republicans is that not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:58.260
is it polarizing, but the country continues to be&nbsp;
more and more and more in favor of abortion being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:04.560
legal in most cases. And in fact, we've we've&nbsp;
seen a steady increase in the Roe v Wade era,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:09.780
which ended a few months ago in Americans,&nbsp;
generally speaking, saying, yeah, in most cases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:15.360
this should be legal. And so when you take that&nbsp;
away, as the Supreme Court did as a right and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:21.000
Republicans can can campaign on how great that is,&nbsp;
it's not necessarily going to be useful to you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:30.720
Secondly, abortion is a distraction, in a sense,&nbsp;
to more pressing issues that Americans feel that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:37.740
they are facing in the sense that Republicans&nbsp;
could be focusing on the economy, health care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:44.460
education and foreign policy without the drag&nbsp;
of their position on abortion being there. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:51.960
what I mean by that is the following. Republicans&nbsp;
tried to make their campaigns to a degree about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:55.860
inflation and the border and Biden's bad and&nbsp;
whatever, and we can adjudicate whether that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.860 --> 00:03:01.620
a good or bad idea on its own. But they were&nbsp;
dragged down by the knowledge and the timing of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:06.900
the Roe v Wade reversal by the Supreme Court. And&nbsp;
so many Republicans saying that that was a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:14.580
great thing. And so that was also a problem for&nbsp;
Republicans. And the maybe last part of this is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:20.220
of course, I'm pro-choice, you all know that.&nbsp;
But the American people are mostly pro-choice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:26.700
And so the Republican position on abortion with&nbsp;
some Republicans saying there should be some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:32.880
exceptions. But putting that aside, it's a flawed&nbsp;
and misguided ideology. And as I mentioned before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:39.600
the position of Republicans is based on the&nbsp;
assumption that abortion is morally wrong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:43.980
it should be banned, it should be illegal.&nbsp;
Maybe it's murder in many cases, regardless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:49.680
of the situation or the circumstances. It ignores&nbsp;
the reality that most Americans don't see it that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:53.760
way. And of course, it ignores the reality that&nbsp;
sometimes abortion is necessary. It's an important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:58.260
option for women to have, and it should not be&nbsp;
something in which the government is involved. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:05.100
on so many levels, this is a misguided strategy.&nbsp;
So what's going to happen next? We saw it backfire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:09.360
at the ballot box in part. There's lots of things&nbsp;
that we can blame for what happened in November,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:14.820
but to a degree, the position on abortion&nbsp;
of the Republican Party was a factor. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:19.620
do they stop talking about abortion? That's&nbsp;
a problem because Republicans still raise a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:24.000
disproportionate amount of money from religious&nbsp;
voters by saying we are against abortion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:29.040
What do they do if they don't talk about it?&nbsp;
There's a risk talking about it. The way they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:36.900
talked about it is also a risk. So in total, the&nbsp;
Republican Party's position on abortion is flawed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:45.960
Medically. It's flawed morally and ethically,&nbsp;
but it's also flawed strategically. If they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:51.540
want to win elections and be successful, they're&nbsp;
going to need to do a rethink. I don't know what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:56.940
that rethink should be. I don't care. I don't&nbsp;
want them to win. So it's not for me to start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:02.880
giving them advice. But we'll have to track&nbsp;
and see whether in some of the 2023 elections,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:08.100
abortion takes on a sort of different tone from&nbsp;
the Republican Party and certainly going into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:13.680
2024. Let me know if you do have thoughts.&nbsp;
How should the Republican Party straddle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:19.080
being against abortion? But the reality that&nbsp;
increasingly the country is in favor of it.

